        {
          "page": "2",
          "section": "2. Material and experiments",
          "quote": "Isothermal forging was conducted on a computer-controlled 500 ton hydraulic press with nickel-base super-alloy flat dies, and an electric resistance heating furnace that was sealed in an argon-filled chamber. The cylindrical cakes were heated to the forging temperature with the tools and were soaked for 2 h. After isothermal forging, samples were removed from the dies and cooled in air. Three isothermal forging schedules: 980 °C (HT-1: α2 + B2 + O phase region), 1040 °C (HT-2: α2 + B2 phase region) and 1060 °C (HT-3: B2 phase region) were used to improve the alloy properties. Isothermal forging was carried out at strain rates of 0.05 s−1. The height reduction of the cylindrical cakes was more than 50% in one stroke.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "7d10e5371277399a:part:001"
        }
      ]
